---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const description =
  'Make your own DeskPet pet: draw a tiny pixel friend, give it tricks and share it with everyone.';
---

<Base title="Make a pet · DeskPet" description={description} path="/contribute/">
  <div class="wrap pt-10">
    <section class="prose">
      <p class="chip">Open to everyone</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-extrabold sm:text-5xl">Make a pet</h1>
      <p class="mt-4 text-lg">
        Every pet on this site is a single small file. Anyone can make one, and good ones get a page
        here next to the rest, credited to you.
      </p>

      <h2>What a pet is</h2>
      <p>
        A pet is a <code>.deskpet</code> file: a tiny WebAssembly module built with the DeskPet pet
        SDK. It holds the pixel art, the walk and sleep poses, and the tricks. DeskPet runs it
        safely in a sandbox, so a pet can only draw itself and react to your desktop.
      </p>

      <h2>The path in three steps</h2>
      <ol class="step-list not-prose mt-4">
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-ink">Read the guide.</strong> The{' '}
            <a href={SITE.contributing}>pet guide (CONTRIBUTING-PETS.md)</a> covers the SDK, the
            file layout and the rules the checker enforces.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-ink">Draw and build.</strong> Start from the template, draw your pet
            and its tricks, then build the <code>.deskpet</code> file and try it on your own
            desktop.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-ink">Send it in.</strong> Open a pull request. Once it is merged,
            your pet gets its own page here with GIFs of every trick and your name on it.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-6">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={SITE.contributing}>
          Read the pet guide
        </a>
      </p>

      <h2>A few house rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Original art only, or art you have the rights to share.</li>
        <li>Keep it friendly: pets live on people's work screens.</li>
        <li>Small is good. Most pets are a 16×16 grid with a handful of colours.</li>
        <li>Every pet must pass the checker before it can join the shelf.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Good companions</h2>
      <p>
        Make something you would like to see out of the corner of your eye all day: a creature with
        a tiny hobby, a hero with one funny flaw, or a legend with a dramatic entrance. The best
        pets do one small thing very well.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
